From 133517a4d27fe025fbc59ad35afd22cc7e4d495e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 08:44:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NET PLAY: a lobby where the host is decided by who picks
 first

The NET PLAY icon (spr_icon frame 318) sits in the gap on the top row
between the map size and the dice - the one 32-wide slot free on every
screen, since MISSION keeps its arrows at x 284 and CUSTOM its dice at
220. It is a toggle, so the same button is the way in and the way out;
EXIT cannot be the way out, because EXIT quits the game. NETPLAY_BTN_X
and NETPLAY_BTN_Y move it.

Everyone with the panel open is a potential host: the TCP door on
NET_PORT is open the whole time, and a UDP beacon goes out on 6511 about
once a second so the others can list you. Nobody declares a role in
advance - pick a machine and you are the client, get picked and you are
the host. That is Bob's model and it is the right one; opening the door
is not the same as claiming to be the host.

Discovery is broadcast plus a manual list. On one LAN the panel fills
itself with no typing. An address added by hand is remembered in
settlers.ini and is never aged out - it was typed on purpose, so one that
is switched off should read "no answer" rather than vanish while you are
looking at it. Broadcast reaches the sender too, so every beacon carries
the session id that machine made at startup, and its own comes back and
is ignored.

The corner that needs a rule is both players picking inside the same
second: each machine then has an outbound connection AND an inbound one,
and if both kept the inbound both would think they were host. The
tie-break is that session id, not the IP address - GameMaker has no call
that says what this machine's own address is, and comparing a number we
do not have against one we do is not a comparison. Both machines have
both ids, because they have been shouting them at each other. Lower id
hosts. If we have never heard the other machine shout - a typed address
that is not broadcasting - there is nothing to compare, so we refuse
their dial and keep our own; if they do the same we both land back in the
lobby with nothing connected, which is visible and recoverable, where two
machines both believing they were host would not be.

The host picks the mission in the lobby, after the join, and presses
START. That is the only order that works: you cannot know whether you
will be host until somebody picks, so choosing beforehand throws the
choice away half the time. The client's START does nothing, correctly -
it has nothing to tell the other machine.

ADD AN ADDRESS gets its own row under the list rather than the
bottom-right slot, which is where LOAD is drawn - a button that reads
LOAD and adds an address is worse than no button. LOAD is not drawn on
this screen at all, since it does nothing here.

F7 and F8 still work and still auto-start mission 1 on connect. The lobby
deliberately does not auto-start, so the two paths do not fight: the
auto-start is now conditional on the lobby being closed.

Two notes on fitting in with the rest of the port. The comma split for
the saved address list is hand-rolled rather than string_split, matching
how update_version_part already avoids it. And the beacon's name field is
sent empty - GameMaker will not tell us the machine's name, and inventing
one from something else would put a made-up label in front of the only
thing that actually identifies the machine. The field stays in the wire
format so a name can be added later without changing it.

